ADD = {5: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 14: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>E.P. Royappa v. State of Tamil Nadu</b> (1974 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A senior IAS officer (Chief Secretary) was moved to a newly created post he said was a demotion.', "<b>Held:</b> Transfer upheld, but Justice Bhagwati's opinion gave the <b>new doctrine</b>: equality is the opposite of <b>arbitrariness</b>; an arbitrary act is unequal and violates Arts 14 and 16."], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Shayara Bano v. Union of India</b> (2017 · 5 judges, 3:2)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A woman divorced by instant triple talaq (talaq-e-biddat) challenged the practice.', '<b>Held:</b> Triple talaq <b>set aside</b>. Justices Nariman and Lalit: it is <b>manifestly arbitrary</b> (marriage broken on a whim, no chance to reconcile), so Sec 2 of the 1937 Shariat Act, as far as it recognised it, violates Art 14. Justice Kurian Joseph: not part of Islam. CJI Khehar and Justice Nazeer dissented (personal law; asked Parliament to legislate).'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Joseph Shine v. Union of India</b> (2018): adultery (Sec 497 IPC) struck down as manifestly arbitrary under Art 14 (also 15, 21)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>State of WB v. Anwar Ali Sarkar (1952)</b>: special courts with unguided discretion violated Art 14; source of the twin test (intelligible differentia + rational nexus). Linked to the Prelims 2021 question on unguided discretion.'), ('mn', 'Class → Whim → Manifest Whim', 'the three tests in order: <b>Class</b> = Anwar Ali Sarkar 1952 (reasonable classification) · <b>Whim</b> = Royappa 1974 (arbitrariness) · <b>Manifest whim</b> = Shayara Bano 2017 (even a law falls). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 15: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>M.R. Balaji v. State of Mysore</b> (1962 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A Mysore order reserved about 68% of seats in engineering and medical colleges for backward classes, SCs and STs.', '<b>Held:</b> Struck down. Reservation must be <b>reasonable</b>, <b>generally below 50%</b>; caste may be one factor but <b>not the only test</b> of backwardness.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Ashoka Kumar Thakur v. Union of India</b> (2008 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> The Central Educational Institutions (Reservation in Admission) Act, 2006 gave <b>27% OBC</b> seats in IITs, IIMs and central universities.', '<b>Held:</b> Both <b>upheld</b> (for State and aided institutions; private unaided left open), but the <b>creamy layer must be excluded</b> from OBC seats. Creamy layer does not apply to SCs/STs.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Tanvi Behl v. Shrey Goel</b> (2025): residence-based reservation in PG medical seats violates Art 14'), ('mn', 'Strike and Patch', 'Court <b>strikes</b>, Parliament <b>patches</b>: Champakam 1951 → 15(4) · Inamdar 2005 → 15(5). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 16: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (Mandal case)</b> (1992 · 9 judges, 6:3)', ["<b>Issue:</b> The V.P. Singh government's 1990 order gave <b>27%</b> of Central jobs to OBCs (Mandal). The 1991 Narasimha Rao order added preference for the poorer OBCs and <b>10%</b> for economically backward among others.", '<b>Held:</b> 27% <b>upheld</b>, with limits: <b>creamy layer</b> excluded; total reservation <b>not above 50%</b> except in extraordinary situations; <b>no reservation in promotions</b> under 16(4) (existing ones for 5 more years; overruled Rangachari, 1962, on this); the 10% economic quota struck down; a permanent body to revise OBC lists (NCBC, 1993). Justices Thommen, Kuldip Singh and R.M. Sahai dissented.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Jarnail Singh v. Lachhmi Narain Gupta</b> (2018 · 5 judges, unanimous)', ['<b>Issue:</b> The Centre asked for Nagaraj to be reviewed by 7 judges.', '<b>Held:</b> No referral. Nagaraj was <b>wrong to require data on SC/ST backwardness</b> (they are presumed backward), but data on <b>inadequate representation</b> and Art 335 remain. <b>Creamy layer applies to SC/ST promotions</b>.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>State of Punjab v. Davinder Singh</b> (2024 · 7 judges, 6:1)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A Punjab Act (2006) gave half of the SC quota first to Balmikis and Mazhabi Sikhs.', '<b>Held:</b> Yes. SCs are not one homogeneous group; sub-classification is allowed on <b>empirical data</b>, open to judicial review, but cannot give all seats to one sub-group or change the Art 341 list. <b>E.V. Chinnaiah (2004) overruled</b>. Justice Bela M. Trivedi dissented. Four judges also suggested a creamy layer for SC/ST (not binding).'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>State of Kerala v. N.M. Thomas</b> (1976): 16(4) is a facet of 16(1), not an exception'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Jarnail Singh (II)</b> (2022): no yardstick fixed for inadequate representation; data per cadre'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>7th Am, 1956</b>: 16(3) reworded to cover a <b>"State or Union territory"</b> (Art 16)'), ('mn', 'Chinnaiah Chained, Davinder Divides', 'sub-classification of SCs: <b>chained</b> (not allowed) in Chinnaiah 2004 · <b>divides</b> (allowed with data) in Davinder Singh 2024. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 17: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Devarajiah v. Padmanna (Mysore High Court)</b> (1958): untouchability means the caste-based practice, not literal touch or every social boycott'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Prathvi Raj Chauhan (2020)</b>: upheld Sec 18A of the SC/ST (POA) Act (no preliminary inquiry or prior approval before arrest).'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Sabarimala (2018)</b>: Justice Chandrachud read excluding menstruating women as a form of untouchability under Art 17.'), ('mn', 'Mahajan Paused, Parliament Restored', '2018: Mahajan <b>paused</b> automatic arrests · Sec 18A <b>restored</b> them; Court recalled (2019) and upheld (Chauhan 2020). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 19: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Romesh Thappar v. State of Madras</b> (1950 · 6 judges, 5:1)', ["<b>Issue:</b> Madras banned the entry of Romesh Thappar's journal <i>Cross Roads</i> under a public-safety law.", '<b>Held:</b> Ban struck down. Free speech includes <b>freedom of circulation</b>; public order is wider than security of the State, the only ground then. Justice Fazl Ali dissented. With <b>Brij Bhushan</b> (1950), it led to the <b>1st Amendment</b>.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Kedar Nath Singh v. State of Bihar</b> (1962 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A party worker was charged with sedition (Sec 124A IPC) for a speech attacking the government.', '<b>Held:</b> Upheld but <b>read down</b>: only words that <b>incite violence</b> or tend to public disorder are sedition; strong criticism of government is not. In 2022 (S.G. Vombatkere) the Court kept 124A in abeyance; the BNS, 2023 dropped it (Sec 152 now covers acts endangering sovereignty).'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Kaushal Kishor v. State of Uttar Pradesh</b> (2023 · 5 judges, 4:1)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A UP minister made remarks about the victims of the Bulandshahr gang-rape (2016).', "<b>Held:</b> <b>The 19(2) grounds are exhaustive</b>; no extra restrictions. A minister's statement is not vicariously the government's. Arts 19 and 21 can be enforced even against <b>non-State actors</b>. Justice B.V. Nagarathna partly dissented (an official statement can be attributed to the government; called for a code of conduct)."], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Brij Bhushan v. State of Delhi</b> (1950): pre-censorship of the weekly <i>Organiser</i> struck down'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Bennett Coleman v. Union of India</b> (1972): newsprint quotas that limit pages curb press freedom'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>1st Am, 1951</b>: 19(2) rewritten: added <b>friendly relations, public order, incitement</b> and the word "reasonable"; 19(6) allows State trade monopolies (after Romesh Thappar) (Art 19)'), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>ADR v. Union of India (Electoral Bonds, 2024)</b>: voters' right to information on political funding is part of Art 19(1)(a); the scheme was struck down."), ('mn', 'Speech through the ages', "<b>Journal</b> = Romesh Thappar 1950 · <b>Rally speech</b> = Kedar Nath 1962 · <b>Film</b> = Rangarajan 1989 · <b>Facebook post</b> = Shreya Singhal 2015 · <b>Internet</b> = Anuradha Bhasin 2020 · <b>Minister's mouth</b> = Kaushal Kishor 2023 · <b>Bond</b> = Electoral Bonds 2024. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>")]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 20: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>State of Bombay v. Kathi Kalu Oghad</b> (1961 · 11 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Accused persons were made to give specimen handwriting and thumb impressions during investigation.', '<b>Held:</b> No. "To be a witness" means <b>imparting personal knowledge</b> (testimony). Thumb impressions, signatures, handwriting samples and showing the body are <b>not</b> self-incrimination.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Maqbool Hussain v. State of Bombay</b> (1953): confiscation by customs is not prosecution; no bar to later criminal trial'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Rattan Lal v. State of Punjab</b> (1965): a later law that benefits the accused can apply retrospectively'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Nandini Satpathy v. P.L. Dani</b> (1978): 20(3) covers police questioning; right to silence'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Ritesh Sinha v. State of Uttar Pradesh</b> (2019): a magistrate can order voice samples; not testimony'), ('mn', "Kathi's Thumb, Selvi's Serum", 'a <b>thumb</b> impression can be taken (Kathi Kalu 1961); a <b>truth serum</b> cannot be forced (Selvi 2010). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 21', 'b'), ('mn', 'Many Unis Ask Rights', 'education chain: <b>M</b>ohini Jain 1992 · <b>U</b>nni Krishnan 1993 · 86th <b>A</b>mendment 2002 · <b>R</b>TE Act 2009')], 'only': 'tb'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 21: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('mn', 'Narrow → Dark → Fair', 'the first three: <b>Gopalan</b> 1950 narrow · <b>ADM Jabalpur</b> 1976 dark · <b>Maneka</b> 1978 fair procedure. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>'), ('mn', "A citizen's life, place by place", '<b>Jail</b> = Hussainara (speedy trial) · <b>Gallows</b> = Bachan Singh (rarest of rare) · <b>Pavement</b> = Olga Tellis (livelihood) · <b>School</b> = Unni Krishnan · <b>Office</b> = Vishaka · <b>Police station</b> = D.K. Basu · <b>Bedroom</b> = Puttaswamy (privacy) · <b>Love</b> = Navtej · <b>Hospital bed</b> = Common Cause (living will) · <b>Sky</b> = Ranjitsinh (climate). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 21A: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Society for Unaided Private Schools of Rajasthan v. Union of India</b> (2012 · 3 judges, 2:1)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Private schools challenged the RTE Act, mainly the <b>25%</b> entry-level seats for weaker and disadvantaged children (Sec 12(1)(c)).', '<b>Held:</b> RTE <b>valid</b>. The 25% quota applies to government, aided and <b>unaided non-minority</b> schools, but not to <b>unaided minority</b> schools (Art 30). Justice K.S. Radhakrishnan dissented (would exempt all unaided schools).'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Pramati Educational and Cultural Trust v. Union of India</b> (2014 · 5 judges)', ["<b>Issue:</b> Challenge to the 86th Amendment (21A) and the 93rd Amendment (15(5)), and to RTE's reach over minority schools.", '<b>Held:</b> Both Amendments <b>valid</b>. The RTE Act does <b>not apply to minority schools, aided or unaided</b>; the 2012 view on aided minority schools was overruled. In 2025 (Anjuman Ishaat-e-Taleem Trust) a 2-judge bench doubted this and asked for a larger bench.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>86th Am, 2002</b>: inserted <b>Art 21A</b>; substituted <b>Art 45</b> (care and education below 6); added duty <b>51A(k)</b> (Art 21A)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Anjuman Ishaat-e-Taleem Trust (2025)</b>: a 2-judge bench doubted Pramati (2014) and referred the RTE-minority schools question to a larger bench.'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Art 21A and the RTE Act</b> came into force on <b>1 April 2010</b>.'), ('mn', 'Unaided Yes, Minority No', '2012: <b>unaided</b> private schools must take the 25% · 2014 (Pramati): <b>all minority</b> schools are outside RTE. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 22: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>A.K. Gopalan v. State of Madras</b> (1950 · 6 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A communist leader was detained under the Preventive Detention Act, 1950.', '<b>Held:</b> The Act was upheld except Sec. 14 (which barred disclosing the grounds to the court). <b>Art. 22 was treated as a complete code</b> for preventive detention, separate from Arts. 19 and 21. Justice Fazl Ali dissented. This separate-boxes view was later rejected (R.C. Cooper 1970, Maneka 1978).'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>A.K. Roy v. Union of India</b> (1982 · 5 judges)', ["<b>Issue:</b> The National Security Act, 1980 was challenged. The 44th Amendment's Sec. 3 (2-month limit, stronger Advisory Boards) had never been brought into force.", '<b>Held:</b> <b>NSA upheld</b>. The Court <b>refused to issue mandamus</b>: Parliament had left the date of commencement to the executive. It added safeguards (detenu to be told grounds, kept near home, may take a friend, not a lawyer, before the Board).'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Mihir Rajesh Shah v. State of Maharashtra</b> (2025)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A man was arrested in the Mumbai BMW hit-and-run case without being given written grounds of arrest.', '<b>Held:</b> <b>Written grounds, in a language the arrested person understands, for every offence under every law</b> (IPC/BNS included). If that is not possible at once, tell him orally and give the written grounds <b>at least 2 hours before</b> he is produced for remand. Non-compliance makes the arrest and remand illegal.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Joginder Kumar v. State of UP</b> (1994): arrest must be justified, not routine; arrested person may have a friend or relative told'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Prabir Purkayastha v. State (NCT of Delhi)</b> (2024): UAPA: written grounds also needed; arrest and remand set aside'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Vihaan Kumar v. State of Haryana</b> (2025): not telling the grounds violates Art. 22(1): arrest illegal, bail must follow even under strict bail laws'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>44th Am, 1978</b>: Sec. 3 would cut the no-Board limit from <b>3 to 2 months</b> and make Boards of serving/retired HC judges (chaired by a serving judge); <b>never brought into force</b>, so the text still says 3 months (A.K. Roy 1982) (Art 22)'), ('mn', 'Written, Told, Tongue', 'the arrest-grounds chain in order: <b>Written</b> = Pankaj Bansal 2023 (PMLA), Purkayastha 2024 (UAPA) · <b>Told</b> = Vihaan Kumar 2025 (not told, arrest illegal) · <b>Tongue</b> = Mihir Shah 2025 (in writing, in his own language, every law). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 25: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Bijoe Emmanuel v. State of Kerala</b> (1986): Jehovah's Witness children who stood but did not sing the anthem were protected (Arts. 19(1)(a) and 25)"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Ismail Faruqui v. Union of India</b> (1994): a mosque is not essential to Islam; State may acquire it (Ayodhya acquisition upheld)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Aishat Shifa v. State of Karnataka (Hijab)</b> (2022): split verdict (1:1) on the school hijab ban; sent to a larger bench')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 29: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>State of Bombay v. Bombay Education Society</b> (1954)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A Bombay order barred pupils whose mother tongue was not English from English-medium schools, hitting Anglo-Indian schools.', '<b>Held:</b> The order was struck down. <b>Art. 29(2) protects every citizen</b>, majority or minority, and a minority school cannot be forced to turn students away by language.'], None, 24)]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 30: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>In re Kerala Education Bill, 1957</b> (1958 · 7 judges (Art. 143 reference))', ["<b>Issue:</b> The President asked whether the Kerala Education Bill's controls on aided schools (teacher appointment, fees) were valid against minority schools.", '<b>Held:</b> Minorities may run institutions of their choice, including general (non-religious) education. The State may set <b>reasonable conditions for aid and recognition</b>, but <b>not conditions that destroy the minority character</b>. A minority is judged with reference to the State whose law is in question.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Ahmedabad St. Xavier's College v. State of Gujarat</b> (1974 · 9 judges): Art. 30 covers general secular education too; controls that take over management struck down"), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>St. Stephen's College v. University of Delhi</b> (1992): an aided minority college may prefer its own community, up to about 50% of seats"), ('mn', 'Pai Places, Inamdar Frees, Pramati Exempts', '<b>Pai</b> places the minority State by State (2002) · <b>Inamdar</b> frees unaided colleges from State quotas (2005) · <b>Pramati</b> exempts minority schools from RTE (2014). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 32: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Romesh Thappar v. State of Madras</b> (1950)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Madras banned the entry of the journal <i>Cross Roads</i> on public-safety grounds. The editor went straight to the Supreme Court.', '<b>Held:</b> Art. 32 is itself a Fundamental Right, and the Supreme Court is the <b>“protector and guarantor”</b> of FRs; it <b>cannot refuse</b> a petition because a High Court remedy exists. The ban violated 19(1)(a), since “public order” was not then a ground in 19(2).'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Rudul Sah v. State of Bihar</b> (1983)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Rudul Sah stayed in jail for 14 years after his acquittal.', '<b>Held:</b> Yes. Release alone is not enough: the Court can order <b>compensation for violation of Art. 21</b> under Art. 32 (Rs 30,000 here).'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Daryao v. State of UP</b> (1961): res judicata applies: a writ dismissed on merits by an HC cannot be refiled under Art. 32 on the same facts'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Nilabati Behera v. State of Orissa</b> (1993): custodial death: compensation in writ jurisdiction; State cannot plead sovereign immunity'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>42nd Am, 1976</b>: inserted <b>Art 32A</b>: in an Art 32 case the SC could not examine a State law unless a Central law was also challenged (Art 32)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>43rd Am, 1977</b>: omitted Art 32A, restoring full Art 32 review (Art 32)'), ('mn', "Romesh's Right, Rudul's Rupees", '<b>Right</b> = Romesh Thappar 1950: Art. 32 is itself a right, SC cannot refuse · <b>Rupees</b> = Rudul Sah 1983: compensation through a writ. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 300A: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>K.T. Plantation v. State of Karnataka</b> (2011 · 5 judges)', ["<b>Issue:</b> Karnataka's 1996 Act acquired the Roerich and Devika Rani estate (about 470 acres near Bengaluru) at fixed, low compensation.", '<b>Held:</b> <b>Public purpose is a precondition</b> for deprivation. Art. 300A does not mention compensation, but a <b>right to compensation is inferred</b>; nil or illusory compensation must be justified and such a law can be tested under Art. 14. The <b>right to property is not part of the basic structure</b>. The Act (protected by Art. 31A) was upheld.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Vidya Devi v. State of Himachal Pradesh</b> (2020 · 2 judges)', ["<b>Issue:</b> In 1967 the State took an illiterate widow's land for a road without acquisition or compensation. She approached the courts decades later.", '<b>Held:</b> No. The <b>right to property is a constitutional right and a human right</b>. A welfare State <b>cannot claim adverse possession</b> against its own citizen, and delay is no bar to a continuing wrong. Compensation was ordered as in an acquisition, with interest.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Kolkata Municipal Corporation v. Bimal Kumar Shah</b> (2024 · 2 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> The corporation took private land using a section of its municipal Act that laid down no acquisition procedure.', '<b>Held:</b> A valid law must also respect <b>seven procedural sub-rights</b>: notice, hearing, reasoned decision, public purpose, fair compensation, efficient and quick process, and conclusion (vesting). The acquisition was quashed.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Jilubhai Nanbhai Khachar v. State of Gujarat</b> (1995): right under Art. 300A is not part of the basic structure'), ('mn', 'Plantation, Path, Procedure', '<b>Plantation</b> = K.T. Plantation (public purpose, compensation not illusory) · <b>Path</b> = Vidya Devi (land taken for a road) · <b>Procedure</b> = Kolkata (seven steps). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 340: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('mn', 'Kaka → Mandal → Rohini', 'The three Art. 340 commissions in order: <b>1953, 1979, 2017</b> (first, second, sub-categorisation). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 341: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Bir Singh v. Delhi Jal Board</b> (2018 · 5 judges): an SC/ST person cannot carry reservation to another State on migrating; status is State-specific (follows Marri Chandra Shekhar Rao, 1990)')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 342: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Bir Singh v. Delhi Jal Board</b> (2018 · 5 judges): ST (and SC) status is State-specific; no reservation claim in another State on migrating')]}], 15: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 21', 'b'), ('mn', 'How Big Mighty Voices Demand Police Law', 'PIL chain: <b>H</b>ussainara · Bandhua · M.C. Mehta · <b>V</b>ishaka · <b>D</b>.K. Basu · Prakash Singh · Lalita Kumari')], 'only': 'tb'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 80', 'b'), ('mn', 'Rajya 2-4-5, Lok 5-4-3', '<b>Rajya = 245</b> (229 + 4 + 12) · <b>Lok = 543</b> (530 + 13): the digits run in opposite order.')], 'only': 'hw'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 80: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>35th Am, 1974</b>: made 80(1) subject to the Tenth Schedule for <b>associate State Sikkim</b> (Art 80)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>36th Am, 1975</b>: removed that reference when Sikkim became a full State (Art 80)'), ('mn', "Kuldip's Open Door", '<b>Open</b> ballot and <b>no domicile</b> rule for the Rajya Sabha, both upheld (2006). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 81: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>31st Am, 1973</b>: raised State seats 500 → <b>525</b>, cut UT seats 25 → <b>20</b>; added the 6 million proviso (Art 81)'), ('mn', '71 → 2000 → 2026', 'freeze on the <b>1971</b> Census: till <b>2000</b> (42nd) → till <b>2026</b> (84th); boundaries on <b>2001</b> (87th). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 85: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Special Reference No. 1 of 2002 (Gujarat)</b> (2002 · 5 judges): the six-month rule (Art 174, the State mirror) applies to a living House, not a dissolved Assembly; elections are the EC's call"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>1st Am, 1951</b>: substituted Art 85: dropped the original rule that the Houses must meet <b>twice a year</b>; kept only the six-month gap rule (Art 85)')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 102: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu</b> (1992 · 5 judges, 3:2)', ["<b>Issue:</b> The anti-defection Tenth Schedule (52nd Am) was challenged, especially para 7, which barred courts from the Speaker's decisions.", '<b>Held:</b> Tenth Schedule <b>valid</b>; para 7 <b>invalid</b> (not ratified by States). The Speaker acts as a <b>tribunal</b>, so his decision is open to <b>judicial review</b>. Minority (Verma, J.) would have struck down the whole Schedule.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Jaya Bachchan v. Union of India</b> (2006)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A Rajya Sabha MP was disqualified for holding the chair of the U.P. Film Development Council, which carried perks.', '<b>Held:</b> Yes. The test is whether the office is <b>capable of yielding pecuniary gain</b>; whether it was actually drawn is <b>irrelevant</b>.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Consumer Education and Research Society v. Union of India</b> (2009): upheld the 2006 law that exempted offices from disqualification retrospectively'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>42nd Am, 1976</b>: s.19 would have narrowed office of profit to offices Parliament declares; never brought into force and omitted by the 44th Am (Art 102)'), ('mn', "Jaya's Chair, Lily's Jail", '<b>Chair</b> = office of profit (Jaya Bachchan 2006) · <b>Jail</b> = conviction disqualifies at once (Lily Thomas 2013). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 103: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Brundaban Nayak v. Election Commission</b> (1965): the EC may hold an inquiry; the Governor (here, the President) must act on its opinion'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>42nd Am, 1976</b>: substituted 103: President decided after only <b>consulting</b> the EC, and also on corrupt-practice disqualifications (Art 103)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>44th Am, 1978</b>: substituted 103 again: back to <b>EC opinion binding</b>, only 102(1) questions (Art 103)'), ('mn', 'President signs, EC decides', "the decision is in the President's name, but the <b>EC's opinion binds</b> (restored by the 44th Am). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>")]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 105: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>P.V. Narasimha Rao v. State (CBI)</b> (1998 · 5 judges, 3:2)', ['<b>Issue:</b> JMM MPs allegedly took bribes to vote against the 1993 no-confidence motion.', '<b>Held:</b> Yes (3:2): MPs who took the bribe <b>and voted</b> were immune; one who took it but did not vote, and the bribe-givers, were not. <b>Overruled in 2024</b>.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Raja Ram Pal v. Speaker, Lok Sabha</b> (2007 · 5 judges, 4:1)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Eleven MPs were expelled after the 2005 cash-for-query sting.', '<b>Held:</b> Expulsion <b>upheld</b>: Parliament may expel for misconduct. But privilege action is <b>not immune from judicial review</b> for gross illegality or violation of rights; mere procedure is not reviewed. Raveendran, J. dissented.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Sita Soren v. Union of India</b> (2024 · 7 judges, unanimous)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A JMM MLA was accused of taking a bribe to vote in the 2012 Rajya Sabha election.', '<b>Held:</b> <b>No</b>. Bribery is not protected; the offence is complete when the bribe is <b>accepted</b>, whether or not the vote is cast. <b>P.V. Narasimha Rao (1998) overruled</b>.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Kalpana Mehta v. Union of India</b> (2018 · 5 judges): courts may refer to parliamentary committee reports, but cannot let parties question them'), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Keshav Singh (Special Reference No. 1 of 1964, decided 1965)</b>: courts can examine whether a legislature's privilege exists; legislatures cannot use privilege to bar judicial review of detention."), ('mn', 'Rao shielded, Soren unshielded', '<b>1998</b> Rao: bribe-takers who voted were immune · <b>2024</b> Soren: <b>no immunity</b> for bribery. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 110: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Rojer Mathew v. South Indian Bank</b> (2019 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> The Finance Act, 2017, passed as a Money Bill, changed tribunal appointments and service conditions.', "<b>Held:</b> The Speaker's decision is <b>not immune from judicial review</b>. Doubting the Aadhaar majority, the Court <b>referred the Money Bill question to a seven-judge bench</b> (still pending). Tribunal Rules, 2017 struck down."], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Mohd. Saeed Siddiqui v. State of U.P.</b> (2014 · 3 judges): had treated the Speaker's Money Bill decision as final and beyond courts; view doubted in 2018-19"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Puttaswamy (Aadhaar, 2018)</b>: the Aadhaar Act upheld as a Money Bill (4:1); Justice Chandrachud dissented, calling it a fraud on the Constitution.'), ('mn', "Aadhaar's Money Mask", 'Aadhaar wore a Money Bill mask: 4 judges said valid, <b>Chandrachud</b> said fraud; <b>Rojer Mathew</b> sent it to 7 judges. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 214: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>7th Am, 1956</b>: omitted the original clauses (1) to (3) tied to Part A and B States, leaving one High Court for each State (common High Courts under the new Art 231) (Art 214)'), ('mn', 'BGP', 'the three High Courts shared by States: <b>B</b>ombay · <b>G</b>auhati · <b>P</b>unjab and Haryana <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 226: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Whirlpool Corporation v. Registrar of Trade Marks</b> (1998)', ['<b>Issue:</b> The Registrar issued a notice without jurisdiction; the High Court refused relief citing an alternative remedy.', '<b>Held:</b> Not where: <b>Fundamental Rights</b> are to be enforced, <b>natural justice</b> is breached, the order is <b>wholly without jurisdiction</b>, or the <b>vires of an Act</b> is challenged.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Andi Mukta Sadguru Trust v. V.R. Rudani</b> (1989)', ["<b>Issue:</b> A trust-run aided college did not pay its teachers' dues.", '<b>Held:</b> Yes, if it performs a <b>public duty</b>: “any person or authority” in Art 226 is wide.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Dwarkanath v. Income Tax Officer</b> (1965): Art 226 is wider than English prerogative writs; the High Court can mould the relief'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>15th Am, 1963</b>: added clause (1A) (now 226(2)): reach based on <b>cause of action</b> (Art 226)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>42nd Am, 1976</b>: curtailed Art 226 and added <b>Art 226A</b> (HCs could not decide validity of Central laws); <b>43rd Am, 1977</b> omitted 226A (Art 226)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>44th Am, 1978</b>: substituted Art 226, restoring “<b>any other purpose</b>” with safeguards on interim orders (Art 226)')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 227: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Waryam Singh v. Amarnath</b> (1954 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A J&K tribunal order was questioned in the High Court under Art. 227.', '<b>Held:</b> It covers <b>judicial as well as administrative</b> superintendence and is wider than the old Sec. 224 of the 1935 Act. But it is to be used <b>sparingly</b>, only to keep subordinate courts <b>within the bounds of their authority</b>, not to correct mere errors.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Radhey Shyam v. Chhabi Nath</b> (2015 · 3 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A party sought a writ of certiorari under Art. 226 against an order of a civil court.', '<b>Held:</b> Judicial orders of civil courts are <b>not open to writ under Art. 226</b>; they can be corrected only under <b>Art. 227</b>. <b>Surya Dev Rai (2003) overruled</b> on this point.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Surya Dev Rai v. Ram Chander Rai</b> (2003): had allowed certiorari under 226 against civil court orders; overruled in 2015'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>42nd Am, 1976</b>: took <b>tribunals</b> out of 227(1) and added a clause (5) so the HC could not question judgments of inferior courts not open to appeal or revision (Art 227)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>44th Am, 1978</b>: <b>restored</b> the original 227(1) (tribunals back in) and dropped clause (5) (Art 227)'), ('mn', 'Headmaster, not examiner', 'Under 227 the HC is a <b>headmaster</b> who keeps lower courts within bounds (Waryam Singh), not an <b>examiner</b> who re-marks every error. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 233: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Chandra Mohan v. State of UP</b> (1966)', ['<b>Issue:</b> UP rules let district judges be picked from officers of the executive branch, and appointments were made without proper consultation with the High Court.', '<b>Held:</b> No. District judges can come only from the <b>judicial service</b> or the <b>Bar</b>, and <b>consultation with the HC is mandatory</b>. The appointments were void; the <b>20th Amendment (Art. 233A)</b> later validated them.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Rejanish K.V. v. K. Deepa</b> (2025 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Serving judicial officers who had earlier practised as advocates applied for the district judge posts kept for the Bar (direct recruitment).', '<b>Held:</b> Yes. A judicial officer with <b>7 years combined experience as advocate and judicial officer</b> is eligible; minimum age <b>35</b> on the date of application. <b>Dheeraj Mor (2020) overruled</b>.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Dheeraj Mor v. High Court of Delhi</b> (2020): had kept the Bar quota only for practising advocates; overruled in 2025'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>20th Am, 1966</b>: inserted <b>Art. 233A</b> to validate district judge appointments struck down in Chandra Mohan (1966) (Art 233)'), ('mn', 'Seven at the Bar, Thirty-five in age', 'Art. 233(2): <b>7 years</b> as advocate (now advocate + judicial officer combined, Rejanish 2025) and <b>35 years</b> of age. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 235: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>State of West Bengal v. Nripendra Nath Bagchi</b> (1966)', ['<b>Issue:</b> The State Government held a disciplinary inquiry against a district-level judicial officer and dismissed him.', "<b>Held:</b> “Control” includes <b>disciplinary jurisdiction</b>. The <b>HC holds the inquiry</b> and decides; the Governor as appointing authority only passes the order of dismissal or removal on the HC's report."], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Shamsher Singh v. State of Punjab</b> (1974 · 7 judges): the HC could not hand an inquiry against a judicial officer to the Government's vigilance department: that gives up its Art. 235 control"), ('mn', 'HC holds the stick, Governor signs the letter', 'Bagchi (1966): the <b>High Court</b> runs discipline; the <b>Governor</b> only signs the removal order. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 265: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Mafatlal Industries v. Union of India</b> (1996 · 9 judges, majority)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Manufacturers claimed refunds of excise duty collected without valid authority of law.', '<b>Held:</b> Tax collected without authority of law must be refunded, but <b>only if the taxpayer has not passed the burden on</b> to buyers (<b>unjust enrichment</b>); claims go through the statutory refund mechanism.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Shirur Mutt (1954)</b> also set the <b>tax vs fee</b> test: a fee needs a quid pro quo (a service in return); a tax does not.')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 267: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Contingency Fund of India</b> corpus raised from Rs 500 crore to <b>Rs 30,000 crore</b> (Finance Act, 2021).')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 330: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>51st Am, 1984</b>: substituted 330(1)(b): ST seats in Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland and Arunachal Pradesh (Art 330)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Lok Sabha reserved seats now:</b> 84 SC and 47 ST.')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 334: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>8th Am, 1959</b>: extended to 1970 (Art 334)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>23rd Am, 1969</b>: to 1980 (Art 334)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>45th Am, 1980</b>: to 1990 (Art 334)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>62nd Am, 1989</b>: to 2000 (Art 334)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>79th Am, 1999</b>: to 2010 (Art 334)'), ('mn', 'Decade ladder: 8 · 23 · 45 · 62 · 79 · 95 · 104', 'one amendment per decade: 1970 · 1980 · 1990 · 2000 · 2010 · 2020 · <b>2030</b>. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}], 7: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 44: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Mohd. Ahmed Khan v. Shah Bano Begum</b> (1985 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A 62-year-old divorced Muslim woman sought maintenance from her husband under Sec. 125 CrPC.', '<b>Held:</b> Yes, <b>Sec. 125 is secular and applies to all</b>; Mahr is not maintenance. The Court regretted that <b>Art. 44 had remained a dead letter</b>.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Ahmedabad Women Action Group v. Union of India</b> (1997): Court declined to strike down personal laws; reform is for the legislature'), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>John Vallamattom v. Union of India</b> (2003): struck Sec. 118 Indian Succession Act (limits on Christians' charitable wills); repeated the call for a UCC"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Jose Paulo Coutinho v. Maria Luiza Valentina Pereira</b> (2019): called Goa a “shining example” of a uniform civil code'), ('mn', 'Bread, Second Wife, Three Words', '<b>Bread</b> = Shah Bano 1985 (maintenance) · <b>Second wife</b> = Sarla Mudgal 1995 (bigamy by conversion) · <b>Three words</b> = Shayara Bano 2017 (instant triple talaq). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 45: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('mn', 'Cradle 45, Classroom 21A, Parent 51A(k)', 'the 86th Amendment (2002) split the old Art. 45 three ways: <b>Cradle</b> = Art. 45, care and education below six · <b>Classroom</b> = Art. 21A, right to education 6-14 · <b>Parent</b> = Art. 51A(k), duty to give the child that chance. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}], 8: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 51A: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Ranganath Mishra v. Union of India</b> (2003): Court asked the Union to act on the Verma Committee (1999) report to teach and spread Fundamental Duties')]}], 10: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 52', 'b'), ('mn', 'Very Careful Succession', 'Vice-President, Chief Justice of India, Senior-most SC Judge (who acts when the post is vacant)')], 'only': 'hw'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 52', 'b'), ('mn', 'Polity Veterans Can Answer', 'President, Vice-President, Council of Ministers (with PM), Attorney-General (the four parts of the Union Executive)')], 'only': 'hw'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 53: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Shamsher Singh v. State of Punjab</b> (1974 · 7 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Two probationary judicial officers were removed; the order was passed by the Council, not by the Governor personally.', '<b>Held:</b> No. The <b>satisfaction of the President or Governor means the satisfaction of the Council of Ministers</b>; they act on aid and advice except where the Constitution gives discretion. Sardari Lal (1971) overruled.'], None, 24), ('mn', "Ram's Remainder, Shamsher's Seal", "<b>Remainder</b> = Ram Jawaya Kapur 1955: executive power is what is left after law-making and judging · <b>Seal</b> = Shamsher Singh 1974: the President only seals the Council's decision. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>")]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 61: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('mn', '14 days, ¼ to start, ⅔ to finish', "<b>14</b> days' notice · <b>¼</b> of the House signs · <b>⅔ of total membership</b> passes it in <b>each</b> House. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>")]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 64: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', "The VP's <b>casting vote</b> comes from <b>Art 100</b>; under <b>Art 92</b> he has no vote at all while a resolution for his own removal is considered."), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>VP events:</b> removal notice against Jagdeep Dhankhar rejected (19 Dec 2024); Dhankhar resigned (21 July 2025); <b>C.P. Radhakrishnan</b> elected 15th VP (9 Sept 2025).'), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Art 67(b) proviso:</b> at least <b>14 days' notice</b> is required before a resolution to remove the VP is moved."), ('mn', 'Chairs, never a Member', 'Not a member, so <b>no vote</b> except the <b>casting vote</b> (Art 100), and none at all in his own removal (Art 92). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 72', 'b'), ('mn', 'Under Court, Death', 'the three cases: <b>U</b>nion law offence · <b>C</b>ourt martial · <b>D</b>eath sentence')], 'only': 'hw'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 72: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('mn', 'Advice → No hearing → Review → Delay', 'the cases in year order: <b>Maru Ram</b> 1980 advice · <b>Kehar</b> 1988 no hearing · <b>Epuru</b> 2006 review grounds · <b>Shatrughan</b> 2014 delay = commute. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 74', 'b'), ('mn', '42nd binds, 44th returns once', '<b>42nd = binding. 44th = may send back once</b>; after the second advice he must act.')], 'only': 'hw'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 74: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Shamsher Singh v. State of Punjab</b> (1974 · 7 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Probationary judicial officers were removed by the Governor on the advice of the State government.', '<b>Held:</b> <b>Satisfaction of the President/Governor = satisfaction of the Council of Ministers</b>, except in a few well-known discretionary cases. The President is a constitutional head. <b>Sardari Lal (1971) overruled</b>.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>S.R. Bommai v. Union of India</b> (1994 · 9 judges): Art 74(2) bars inquiry into the advice, but not into the material on which the advice was based'), ('mn', "Shamsher's Satisfaction", "the President's satisfaction is really the <b>Cabinet's satisfaction</b> (Shamsher Singh 1974). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>")]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 75: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>S.P. Anand v. H.D. Deve Gowda</b> (1996 (handout: 1997))', ['<b>Issue:</b> H.D. Deve Gowda was sworn in as PM in 1996 while not a member of either House.', '<b>Held:</b> Yes. A non-member can be PM, but must become a member of <b>either House within six months</b> (Art 75(5)), or he ceases to hold office.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>S.R. Chaudhuri v. State of Punjab</b> (2001)', ["<b>Issue:</b> A non-member was made minister in Punjab, failed to get elected within six months, resigned, and was reappointed in the same Assembly's term.", '<b>Held:</b> <b>No</b>. Repeated reappointment of a non-member without election is a <b>fraud on the Constitution</b>; the six-month window is a one-time exception (Art 164(4), same rule as 75(5)).'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Manoj Narula v. Union of India</b> (2014 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A PIL sought to stop persons facing serious criminal charges from being appointed ministers.', '<b>Held:</b> No; the Court cannot add disqualifications. But the PM (and CM) is expected, as a matter of <b>constitutional trust</b>, not to choose persons charged with heinous or corruption offences.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Har Sharan Verma v. Tribhuvan Narain Singh</b> (1971): a non-member can be appointed Chief Minister (the six-month rule); the base for S.P. Anand'), ('mn', 'Fifteen, no Floor-crossers', '91st Am: <b>15%</b> cap (75(1A)) and <b>no defector</b> as minister (75(1B)). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 111', 'b'), ('mn', 'People Must Know Zail', "veto examples: <b>P</b>EPSU 1954, <b>M</b>Ps' salary 1991 (absolute) · <b>K</b>alam 2006 (suspensive) · <b>Z</b>ail Singh 1986 (pocket)")], 'only': 'hw'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 111: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Presidential Reference (20 Nov 2025, 5 judges)</b>: courts cannot fix timelines for the Governor or President on State Bills and there is no "deemed assent"; this replaces the April 2025 Tamil Nadu timelines.')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 123', 'b'), ('mn', 'Never Neglect Constitutional Limits', 'four limits: <b>N</b>ot in session · <b>N</b>ecessity · <b>C</b>ompetence · <b>L</b>aid before Parliament')], 'only': 'hw'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 123: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>D.C. Wadhwa v. State of Bihar</b> (1986 (handout: 1987) · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Bihar kept 256 ordinances alive for 1 to 14 years (1967-81) by re-issuing them without placing them before the legislature.', '<b>Held:</b> <b>Re-promulgation</b> without legislative approval is a <b>fraud on the Constitution</b> and subverts the democratic process.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Krishna Kumar Singh v. State of Bihar</b> (2017 · 7 judges; Lokur, J. dissented)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Bihar issued ordinances taking over Sanskrit schools; they were re-promulgated and never laid before the legislature.', '<b>Held:</b> <b>Laying is mandatory</b>; failure is a serious constitutional breach. Re-promulgation is a <b>fraud</b>. Satisfaction is <b>justiciable</b>. Effects survive only if <b>irreversible</b> or undoing them would harm the public interest.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>T. Venkata Reddy v. State of A.P.</b> (1985): an ordinance cannot be struck down for improper motive, just like an Act'), ('mn', 'Lay it or lose it', 'Krishna Kumar Singh 2017: an ordinance <b>must be laid</b>; re-issuing it again is a fraud. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}], 40: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 76', 'b'), ('mn', 'Attorneys Speak Politely', '<b>A</b>ppointment Art 76 · <b>S</b>peak (no vote) Art 88 · <b>P</b>rivileges Art 105')], 'only': 'hw'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 76', 'b'), ('mn', 'Courts Judge All Experts', 'qualifications: <b>C</b>itizen · <b>J</b>udge of HC 5 yrs · <b>A</b>dvocate of HC 10 yrs · <b>E</b>minent jurist')], 'only': 'hw'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 165', 'b'), ('mn', 'MIRROR', 'State posts mirror the Union: <b>76 to 165</b> (AG to Advocate-General) · 88 to 177 · 105 to 194(2)')], 'only': 'tb'}], 14: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 124: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>15th Am, 1963</b>: inserted <b>124(2A)</b>: age of a judge decided as Parliament provides (Art 124)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>NJAC case (2015)</b>: decided 4:1; Justice J. Chelameswar dissented.')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 131: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>State of West Bengal v. Union of India</b> (1962 · 6 judges, 5:1)', ["<b>Issue:</b> First Art 131 suit. Parliament's Coal Bearing Areas (Acquisition and Development) Act, 1957 let the Centre acquire coal-bearing land owned by West Bengal.", "<b>Held:</b> Yes, Parliament can. <b>States are not sovereign</b>; the Union's power of acquisition reaches State property. Suit dismissed. Justice K. Subba Rao dissented."], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>State of Rajasthan v. Union of India</b> (1977 · 7 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> After the 1977 Lok Sabha election, the Union Home Minister asked nine Congress-ruled States to dissolve their Assemblies; the States sued.', "<b>Held:</b> The Court heard the suits but <b>refused relief</b>: the President's satisfaction under Art 356 is largely political and courts step in only for mala fide or wholly extraneous grounds."], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>State of West Bengal v. Union of India</b> (2024 · 2 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> West Bengal withdrew general consent for the CBI (DSPE Act, s. 6) in 2018, but the CBI kept registering cases. The Union said the CBI is independent, not the “Union”.', "<b>Held:</b> Yes. The CBI works under the <b>Union's superintendence</b> under the DSPE Act, so this is a State-Union dispute over a legal right; the suit goes to trial on merits."], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>State of Karnataka v. Union of India</b> (1977): Centre's Commission of Inquiry into charges against a sitting Chief Minister upheld"), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>State of Kerala v. Union of India</b> (2024): Kerala's suit on the net borrowing ceiling: Art 293 questions referred to a 5-judge bench; interim relief refused"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>7th Am, 1956</b>: substituted the <b>proviso</b> (pre-Constitution treaties and similar instruments excluded) (Art 131)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>42nd Am, 1976</b>: added <b>Art 131A</b>: SC alone to decide validity of Central laws; <b>43rd Am, 1977</b> omitted it (Art 131)'), ('mn', 'Bengal sued twice: Coal, then CBI', '<b>Coal</b> land 1962 (States not sovereign) · <b>CBI</b> consent 2024 (suit maintainable) <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 136: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Pritam Singh v. The State</b> (1950)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A convict in a murder case sought special leave after his appeal failed.', '<b>Held:</b> Only in <b>exceptional and special circumstances</b>, where <b>substantial and grave injustice</b> has been done, using the same standard in all cases.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Kunhayammed v. State of Kerala</b> (2000 · 3 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> After the SC dismissed an SLP against a High Court order, the question was whether the High Court could still review that order.', '<b>Held:</b> No. Dismissal <b>at the leave stage</b> (non-speaking order) is not a decision on merits: <b>no merger</b> and <b>no law declared under Art 141</b>. Merger happens only once leave is granted and the appeal is decided.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Durga Shankar Mehta v. Raghuraj Singh</b> (1954): special leave lies even against an Election Tribunal's decision"), ('mn', 'Leave, not a Right', "SLP is the Court's <b>leave</b> (discretion), not a right of appeal: <b>any court except the Army's</b> <i>(own mnemonic)</i>")]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 137: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>P.N. Eswara Iyer v. Registrar, Supreme Court</b> (1980): deciding review petitions by circulation, without oral hearing, is valid'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Mohd. Arif v. Registrar, Supreme Court</b> (2014 · 5 judges): review in death-sentence cases must be heard orally in open court'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Kantaru Rajeevaru v. Indian Young Lawyers Association (Sabarimala review)</b> (2019; 2020 (9 judges)): wider religion questions sent to a larger bench in review; the 9-judge bench held such a reference is possible')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 141: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Central Board of Dawoodi Bohra Community (2005)</b>: a smaller bench is bound by a larger bench; a bench of equal strength that doubts a ruling must refer it to a larger bench.')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 142: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Union Carbide Corporation v. Union of India</b> (1991 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Review of the 1989 Bhopal gas settlement (US$ 470 million) recorded by the Court, which had also quashed the criminal cases.', '<b>Held:</b> Settlement upheld but <b>criminal cases revived</b>. <b>Prohibitions in ordinary laws cannot by themselves limit Art 142</b>, though it must respect basic public policy.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Supreme Court Bar Association v. Union of India</b> (1998 · 5 judges)', ["<b>Issue:</b> In a contempt case (Vinay Chandra Mishra, 1995) the Court had suspended a senior advocate's licence to practise.", '<b>Held:</b> No. Art 142 is <b>supplementary: it cannot supplant substantive law</b> or build a new structure where none exists. Only the Bar Council can suspend a licence; Vinay Chandra Mishra overruled on this point.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Shilpa Sailesh v. Varun Sreenivasan</b> (2023 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Whether the SC can directly dissolve a marriage that has broken down.', '<b>Held:</b> Yes: divorce on <b>irretrievable breakdown</b>, and the 6-month wait for mutual-consent divorce can be waived. Art 142 is not bound by statute but must respect <b>fundamental public policy</b>; no one can claim it as a right through Art 32.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Prem Chand Garg v. Excise Commissioner, UP</b> (1962): Art 142 orders cannot violate Fundamental Rights'), ('mn', 'Gas, Mosque land, Divorce, Ballot, Bills', 'five uses of complete justice: <b>Gas</b> = Bhopal (1991) · <b>Mosque land</b> = Ayodhya (2019) · <b>Divorce</b> = Shilpa Sailesh (2023) · <b>Ballot</b> = Chandigarh Mayor (2024) · <b>Bills</b> = Tamil Nadu (2025, later limited) <i>(own mnemonic)</i>'), ('mn', 'Supplement, not Supplant', 'SCBA (1998): Art 142 fills gaps but cannot replace the law <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 143: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Dr. M. Ismail Faruqui v. Union of India</b> (1994 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> The 1993 reference asked whether a Hindu temple stood at the site before the Babri Masjid was built.', '<b>Held:</b> No. The Court <b>respectfully declined</b> to answer, as the reference was superfluous once the title suits were revived.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>In re Natural Resources Allocation (Special Reference No. 1 of 2012)</b> (2012 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> After the 2G judgment (2012) cancelled 122 licences, the President asked whether auction is compulsory for all natural resources.', '<b>Held:</b> No. <b>Auction is not the only constitutional method</b>; Art 14 needs fair, non-arbitrary allocation for the common good. The 2G ruling was confined to spectrum.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>16th Presidential Reference (2025)</b> under Art 143: on the Governor and President acting on State Bills (opinion 20 Nov 2025).')]}], 39: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 148', 'b'), ('mn', 'A T S', '<b>A</b>ct 1971 · <b>T</b>erm 6 years · <b>S</b>ixty-five years of age: not in the Constitution')], 'only': 'tb'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 148: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>AUTSPI v. Union of India (2014)</b>: CAG can audit the revenue accounts of private telecom companies that share revenue with the government.')]}], 11: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 153: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('mn', 'Seventh = two States', 'the <b>7th</b> Amendment let one Governor serve <b>two or more States</b> <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 155', 'b'), ('mn', 'No Poll Means Less Strife', 'five reasons against an elected Governor: Nominal head · Potential friction · Manifesto without authority · Loss of central control · Second-rank candidate')], 'only': 'tb'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 161: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>State of Haryana v. Raj Kumar</b> (2021): Governor, on State advice, can release a life convict before 14 years: Art 161 overrides Sec 433A'), ('mn', 'Ministers, Errors, Cabinet', '<b>Maru Ram</b> = Ministers advise · <b>Epuru</b> = Errors are reviewable · <b>Perarivalan</b> = Cabinet binds the Governor <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 163', 'b'), ('mn', 'Different But similar', 'Governor has <b>D</b>iscretion; President is <b>B</b>ound by advice since the 42nd Am')], 'only': 'tb'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 163: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>M.P. Special Police Establishment v. State of M.P.</b> (2004 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> The Lokayukta found a case against two State Ministers; the Cabinet refused sanction to prosecute; the Governor granted it.', "<b>Held:</b> Yes, where the Cabinet's advice is <b>biased or irrational</b> (Ministers judging colleagues), the Governor can grant <b>sanction to prosecute</b> on his own."], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Subhash Desai v. Principal Secretary, Governor of Maharashtra</b> (2023 · 5 judges, unanimous)', ['<b>Issue:</b> During the 2022 Shiv Sena split the Governor asked the Thackeray government to face a floor test.', "<b>Held:</b> No: he had <b>no objective material</b> that the government had lost the House's confidence, and an intra-party feud is not a ground. But the government could not be restored because the CM had <b>resigned</b> without facing the test."], None, 24)]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 201: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Hoechst Pharmaceuticals v. State of Bihar</b> (1983): the President's assent and the Governor's reservation are not open to judicial review"), ('mn', 'April Clock, November No-Clock', '<b>April 2025</b>: Tamil Nadu judgment sets a clock (timelines, deemed assent) · <b>November 2025</b>: the Reference removes the clock <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 213: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Krishna Kumar Singh v. State of Bihar</b> (2017 · 7 judges, majority)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A Bihar ordinance taking over 429 Sanskrit schools was re-promulgated several times and never laid; staff claimed rights under it.', "<b>Held:</b> Re-promulgation is a fraud on the Constitution; <b>laying before the Legislature is mandatory</b>; the Governor's <b>satisfaction is judicially reviewable</b>; rights under a lapsed ordinance do <b>not survive automatically</b>."], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>38th Am, 1975</b>: added 213(4): Governor's satisfaction <b>final, not justiciable</b> (Art 213)"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>44th Am, 1978</b>: deleted 213(4): satisfaction is open to judicial review again (Art 213)')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 361: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Rameshwar Prasad v. Union of India</b> (2006 · 5 judges, 3:2)', ["<b>Issue:</b> The Bihar Governor's report led to dissolution of the Assembly in 2005; the Governor's conduct was attacked as mala fide.", '<b>Held:</b> The Governor enjoys <b>complete personal immunity</b>: he cannot be made to answer in court. But the <b>action itself can be reviewed</b>, even for mala fides; the Union must defend it.'], None, 24)]}], 12: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 163', 'b'), ('mn', 'All Act Closely', '<b>A</b>id and advice 163 · <b>A</b>ppointment and tenure 164 · <b>C</b>ommunication duties of CM 167')], 'only': 'tb'}], 17: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 169: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Andhra Pradesh</b> passed a resolution to abolish its Legislative Council in 2020 and withdrew it in 2021.')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 332: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>R.C. Poudyal v. Union of India</b> (1993 · 5 judges): Sikkim's seat for the Sangha (monasteries) and Bhutia-Lepcha seats upheld under Art. 371F, a departure from the 332 pattern"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>57th Am, 1987</b>: inserted <b>332(3A)</b> for the four North-East States (Art 332)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>72nd Am, 1992</b>: inserted <b>332(3B)</b> for Tripura (Art 332)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>90th Am, 2003</b>: proviso to 332(6): <b>Bodoland Territorial Areas District</b> keeps its existing ST and non-ST representation (Art 332)')]}], 26: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 239AA: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>State (NCT of Delhi) v. Union of India</b> (2018 · 5 judges, unanimous (3 opinions))', ['<b>Issue:</b> The elected Delhi Government and the LG clashed over who runs Delhi.', '<b>Held:</b> Yes, <b>the LG is bound by aid and advice</b> except on public order, police and land and where a law gives him discretion. The proviso (“any matter”) is for exceptional cases, <b>not every matter</b>. Delhi is not a State, but the elected government must have real power (<b>collaborative federalism</b>).'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Government of NCT of Delhi v. Union of India (Services)</b> (2023 · 5 judges, unanimous)', ['<b>Issue:</b> After a split 2019 ruling, the question of control over civil servants in Delhi went to a Constitution Bench.', '<b>Held:</b> The <b>Delhi Government controls services</b>, except those linked to public order, police and land; the LG is bound by its advice here. Reason: the <b>triple chain of accountability</b> (officers → ministers → Assembly → voters).'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Government of NCT of Delhi v. Office of the LG (Aldermen)</b> (2024 · 3 judges)', ["<b>Issue:</b> The LG nominated 10 aldermen to the Municipal Corporation of Delhi without the Delhi Government's advice.", '<b>Held:</b> No. The power comes from a <b>statute</b> (Delhi Municipal Corporation Act, 1957, as amended in 1993) that gives it to the LG himself, so he acts <b>in his own discretion</b>.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>NDMC v. State of Punjab</b> (1996 · 9 judges): Delhi, even after the 69th Amendment, remains a Union Territory, not a State'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Government of NCT of Delhi v. Union of India</b> (2019 · 2 judges, split): split on services (Sikri vs Bhushan); sent to a larger bench, settled in 2023'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>LG v. GNCTD (2024)</b>: the Lieutenant Governor nominates MCD aldermen on his own, without the aid and advice of the Delhi Council of Ministers.'), ('mn', 'Advice, Services, Aldermen', 'The three rulings in order: <b>2018 Advice</b> binds the LG · <b>2023 Services</b> go to Delhi · <b>2024 Aldermen</b> stay with the LG. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}], 13: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 243K: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>State of Goa v. Fouziya Imtiaz Shaikh</b> (2021 · 3 judges)', ["<b>Issue:</b> Goa gave the State Election Commissioner's charge to its own Law Secretary, a serving government officer.", '<b>Held:</b> No. The SEC must be an <b>independent person</b>; a person holding an office under the Central or State Government cannot be made SEC. Doing so is a <b>mockery of the constitutional mandate</b>.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Kishansing Tomar v. Municipal Corporation of Ahmedabad</b> (2006 · 5 judges): elections must be held <b>before the term ends</b>; the State must obey the SEC's directions (Art. 243U/243ZA)"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Suresh Mahajan v. State of MP</b> (2022): SEC told to notify overdue local body polls; delimitation or OBC data cannot be used to delay elections'), ('mn', "The umpire can't be on the payroll", 'Fouziya (2021): a <b>government officer cannot be the SEC</b>. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 243ZD: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('mn', 'District 4/5, Metro 2/3', 'The <b>bigger fraction goes to the District</b>, the smaller to the Metro. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}], 20: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 246: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('mn', "Balsara's Bottle, Miner's Money", '<b>Bottle</b> = liquor ban (Balsara 1951): pith and substance. <b>Money</b> = mining royalty is not a tax (MADA 2024). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>'), ('mn', 'Jungle, School, Shop, Court', "42nd Am's five shifts to Concurrent: <b>Jungle</b> = forests + wildlife · <b>School</b> = education · <b>Shop</b> = weights and measures · <b>Court</b> = administration of justice. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>")]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 248: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('mn', "Dhillon's Dhan", '<b>Dhan</b> = wealth: wealth tax on farm land was valid under residuary power. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 254: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Zaverbhai Amaidas v. State of Bombay</b> (1954 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A Bombay Act raised the punishment for breaking food-grain orders; a later Central Act (1950) on the Essential Supplies Act fixed a lower one.', "<b>Held:</b> Yes. Both laws dealt with the <b>same matter</b> (punishment for breaking such orders), so the <b>later Parliament law prevailed</b> over the State's enhanced punishment."], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Deep Chand v. State of UP</b> (1959)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A UP law nationalising bus routes clashed with a later Central amendment to the Motor Vehicles Act.', '<b>Held:</b> Laid down <b>three tests</b>: (1) <b>direct conflict</b> between the provisions; (2) Parliament meant to make an <b>exhaustive code</b>; (3) both laws <b>occupy the same field</b>.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>M. Karunanidhi v. Union of India</b> (1979)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Former TN Chief Minister said the TN Public Men (Criminal Misconduct) Act, 1973 was repugnant to the IPC and the Prevention of Corruption Act.', '<b>Held:</b> No. Repugnancy needs an <b>irreconcilable conflict</b>; the State Act could stand with the Central laws. Prosecution continued; a <b>Chief Minister is a public servant</b>.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Kaiser-I-Hind v. National Textile Corporation</b> (2002 · 5 judges, majority)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Occupants relied on the Bombay Rents Act (which had Presidential assent) against eviction under the Central Public Premises Act, 1971.', "<b>Held:</b> No. Assent protects the State law <b>only against the Central laws named</b> in the State's proposal. The Public Premises Act prevailed."], None, 24), ('tree', "<b>Forum for People's Collective Efforts v. State of West Bengal</b> (2021 · 2 judges)", ['<b>Issue:</b> West Bengal made its own housing law (WB-HIRA, 2017) almost copying the Central <b>RERA, 2016</b>, without Presidential assent.', '<b>Held:</b> No. WB-HIRA was <b>struck down as repugnant to RERA</b> (Art. 254(1)). The Court restated the three tests and said a State cannot enact a law that covers the same field and creates a parallel regime.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>T. Barai v. Henry Ah Hoe</b> (1983): later Central amendment (1976) to the Food Adulteration Act prevailed over West Bengal's harsher amendment; accused got the lighter punishment"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Innoventive Industries v. ICICI Bank</b> (2017): Maharashtra Relief Undertakings Act repugnant to the later <b>IBC, 2016</b>; IBC prevails (254(1))'), ('mn', 'Clash, Code, Copy', "Deep Chand's three tests: <b>Clash</b> = direct conflict · <b>Code</b> = Parliament made a complete code · <b>Copy</b> = both cover the same field (WB-HIRA copied RERA). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>"), ('mn', 'A shield with a name tag', 'Presidential assent is a shield that protects <b>only against the Central law named</b> (Kaiser-I-Hind), and Parliament can still <b>break it later</b> (proviso, Zaverbhai). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 270: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>101st Am, 2016</b>: inserted <b>(1A) and (1B)</b> for GST; dropped the 268A reference (268A omitted) (Art 270)')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 292: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('mn', 'Centre: World · State: Home', 'The <b>Centre</b> borrows from the whole <b>world</b> (292); a <b>State</b> borrows only at <b>home</b>, inside India (293). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 293: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>State of Kerala v. Union of India</b> (2024 · 2 judges, referred to a 5-judge Constitution Bench)', ['<b>Issue:</b> The Centre imposed a net borrowing ceiling on Kerala that also counted borrowings of State-owned enterprises and public-account liabilities. Kerala filed an original suit under Art. 131.', "<b>Held:</b> <b>Interim relief refused</b>: no prima facie case, balance of convenience with the Union, and the hardship was largely the State's own doing. Since Art. 293 has <b>never been authoritatively interpreted</b>, the questions were <b>referred to a Constitution Bench</b> under Art. 145(3); the reference was still pending at last check."], None, 24), ('mn', 'Owe the Centre? Ask the Centre.', '293(3): while a Central loan or guarantee is outstanding, a new State loan needs <b>Union consent</b>. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 310: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Art 310</b>', ["Doctrine of pleasure: members of the defence services, Union civil services and All-India Services hold office at the President's pleasure; State civil servants at the Governor's pleasure. In India the pleasure is a constitutional power, limited by Art. 311 and used on ministerial advice. Offices with fixed tenure (judges, CAG, CEC, PSC members) are outside it.", "<b>310(1)</b>: Defence, Union civil posts and All-India Services: <b>President's pleasure</b>; State civil posts: <b>Governor's pleasure</b>", '<b>310(2)</b>: A contract with a specially qualified person may give <b>compensation</b> if the post is abolished or he must leave early for reasons other than misconduct'], None, 22), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Shamsher Singh v. State of Punjab</b> (1974 · 7 judges): pleasure under Art. 310 is exercised on the Council of Ministers' advice (Sardari Lal, 1971, overruled)"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Union of India v. Tulsiram Patel</b> (1985 · 5 judges, 4:1): Indian pleasure doctrine is subject only to Art. 311 and the Constitution (full case under Art. 311)'), ('mn', 'Pleasure with a Shield', "<b>310</b> = pleasure (you serve at the President's pleasure) · <b>311</b> = the shield that limits it. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>")]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 312: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>42nd Am, 1976</b>: added the <b>All-India Judicial Service</b> to 312(1) and inserted clauses (3) and (4) (Art 312)'), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>28th Am, 1972</b>: inserted <b>Art 312A</b>: Parliament may vary or end the special service conditions of former Secretary of State's (ICS) officers (Art 312)"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Indian Forest Service</b> created in <b>1966</b> under Art 312.')]}], 21: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 262: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>In re Cauvery Water Disputes Tribunal</b> (1991 · Presidential reference (Art. 143))', ["<b>Issue:</b> Karnataka passed an Ordinance to nullify the Cauvery Tribunal's interim order to release water to Tamil Nadu.", "<b>Held:</b> No. The Ordinance was <b>unconstitutional</b>: a State cannot nullify a tribunal's order made under Parliament's law. The tribunal can grant <b>interim relief</b>."], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>2016 (Cauvery):</b> appeals under Art 136 against inter-State water tribunal awards are maintainable.'), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Mullaperiyar (2014)</b>: Kerala's law capping the dam level was struck down; Tamil Nadu may raise the level to 142 ft.")]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 301: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Atiabari Tea Co. v. State of Assam</b> (1961 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Assam taxed tea carried by road or inland waterway through the State on its way to Calcutta.', "<b>Held:</b> Yes. Art. 301 strikes at restrictions that <b>directly and immediately</b> impede the movement of trade, and a tax can be one. The Act lacked the President's sanction under Art. 304(b) and was struck down."], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Jindal Stainless v. State of Haryana</b> (2016 · 9 judges, 7:2)', ['<b>Issue:</b> States levied entry tax on goods brought in from other States.', '<b>Held:</b> <b>Taxes are not by themselves restrictions</b> under Art. 301; only <b>discriminatory</b> taxes are barred by Art. 304(a). The <b>compensatory tax theory was rejected</b> and Atiabari and Automobile Transport overruled to that extent. Justices D.Y. Chandrachud and Ashok Bhushan dissented.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Automobile Transport (Rajasthan) v. State of Rajasthan</b> (1962 · 7 judges): regulatory and compensatory taxes (for roads) do not hit Art. 301; overruled on this in 2016'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>4th Am, 1955</b>: substituted Art 305: laws creating <b>State monopolies</b> are saved from Art 301 (Art 301)'), ('mn', "Atiabari's Tea, Jindal's Steel", '<b>Tea</b> (1961): a tax can block trade · <b>Steel</b> (2016): only a <b>discriminatory</b> tax blocks trade. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}], 34: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 279A: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Union of India v. Mohit Minerals</b> (2022 · 3 judges)', ["<b>Issue:</b> Importers challenged IGST on ocean freight that the Centre levied by notification on the GST Council's recommendation.", "<b>Held:</b> No. They have only <b>persuasive value</b>; Parliament and State legislatures have <b>simultaneous power</b> under Art. 246A. The levy on ocean freight was struck down. India's federalism is a <b>dialogue</b> (cooperative and uncooperative)."], None, 24), ('mn', "Council counsels, doesn't command", 'Mohit Minerals (2022): GST Council advice is <b>persuasive, not binding</b>. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}], 58: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 311: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Art 311</b>', ['Two shields for civil servants: (1) no dismissal or removal by an authority subordinate to the appointing authority; (2) no dismissal, removal or reduction in rank without an inquiry where the charges are told and a reasonable opportunity of being heard is given. Only these three major penalties are covered. Since the 42nd Amendment there is no second hearing on the proposed penalty.', '<b>311(1)</b>: Not dismissed or removed by an authority <b>subordinate to the one that appointed</b> him', '<b>311(2)</b>: Dismissal, removal or reduction in rank only after an <b>inquiry</b>: informed of charges + reasonable opportunity; penalty may be imposed on the inquiry evidence, <b>no further representation on penalty</b>', '<b>311(2) 2nd proviso</b>: No inquiry needed: <b>(a)</b> conduct that led to <b>conviction on a criminal charge</b> · <b>(b)</b> authority records in writing that an inquiry is <b>not reasonably practicable</b> · <b>(c)</b> President or Governor satisfied it is not expedient for the <b>security of the State</b>', "<b>311(3)</b>: The authority's decision under (b) that an inquiry is not practicable is <b>final</b>"], None, 22), ('tree', '<b>Parshotam Lal Dhingra v. Union of India</b> (1958 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A railway officer officiating in a higher post was reverted to his substantive post after adverse remarks.', '<b>Held:</b> Art. 311 protects only against <b>punitive</b> dismissal, removal or reduction. Two tests: did the servant have a <b>right to the post</b>, or does the order bring <b>evil consequences</b> (loss of pay, seniority, stigma)? Reverting an officiating officer is not a penalty.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Union of India v. Tulsiram Patel</b> (1985 · 5 judges, 4:1)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Many government servants were dismissed without any inquiry under the second proviso to Art. 311(2).', '<b>Held:</b> No. Where a ground in the second proviso applies, <b>audi alteram partem is excluded</b>; the pleasure doctrine is subject only to Art. 311. But reasons must be recorded and the action is open to <b>judicial review</b>, and departmental appeals remain. Challappan (1975) overruled on this point. Justice M.P. Thakkar dissented.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Union of India v. Mohd. Ramzan Khan</b> (1990): first held the inquiry report must be supplied (applied prospectively; affirmed in Karunakar)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Moti Ram Deka v. General Manager, N.E.F. Railway</b> (1963): terminating a permanent servant under service rules is “removal”, so Art. 311 applies'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>15th Am, 1963</b>: substituted 311(2) and (3): inquiry with notice of charges; the second chance was limited to the penalty, on the inquiry evidence (Art 311)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>42nd Am, 1976</b>: removed the <b>second opportunity</b> (representation against the proposed penalty) (Art 311)'), ('mn', "Crime, Can't, Country", "no inquiry needed: <b>Crime</b> = conviction (a) · <b>Can't</b> = inquiry not practicable (b) · <b>Country</b> = security of the State (c). <i>(own mnemonic)</i>"), ('mn', 'Post or Pain', "Dhingra's two tests: a <b>right to the post</b>, or <b>evil consequences</b> (pain) for the servant. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>")]}], 31: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 317: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>In re Mehar Singh Saini, Chairman, HPSC</b> (2010 · 3 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> The President referred charges against the Haryana PSC Chairman and members: irregular and favoured selections, a bogus certificate, and refusing to cooperate with a vigilance probe.', '<b>Held:</b> “Misbehaviour” is <b>wide</b>: conduct not expected of a constitutional office-holder that erodes public faith in the office. The inquiry is <b>sui generis</b> (neither criminal nor service), so the standard is <b>preponderance of probabilities</b>, not proof beyond reasonable doubt. Misbehaviour was found and removal advised.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>In re Mepung Tadar Bage, Member, Arunachal Pradesh PSC</b> (2025 · 2 judges): misbehaviour must be <b>personal</b> and shown by cogent material; the Commission's collective failure in a paper leak is not enough; member cleared"), ('mn', 'Broke, Busy, Broken', '317(3), President removes directly: <b>Broke</b> = insolvent · <b>Busy</b> = paid work outside · <b>Broken</b> = infirm. Only misbehaviour goes to the Court. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 320: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>State of U.P. v. Manbodhan Lal Srivastava</b> (1957)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A government officer was reduced in rank without the Public Service Commission being consulted as Art. 320(3)(c) requires.', '<b>Held:</b> No. Art. 320(3) is <b>directory, not mandatory</b>; skipping consultation does not void the order, and it gives the servant <b>no enforceable right</b>.'], None, 24)]}], 30: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 324: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>T.N. Seshan v. Union of India</b> (1995 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A 1993 ordinance (later an Act) made the Commission multi-member with equal say; CEC Seshan challenged it.', '<b>Held:</b> Yes. A <b>multi-member Commission is valid</b>; the CEC is <b>first among equals</b> and <b>decisions go by majority</b>. His removal protection does not make him superior to the ECs.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>A.C. Jose v. Sivan Pillai</b> (1984): Art. 324 cannot override a statute: EVM use without law set aside (RPA amended in 1988)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>ADR v. ECI (VVPAT, 2024)</b>: plea for 100% VVPAT slip counting or a return to paper ballots rejected; EVM-VVPAT system upheld with added safeguards.'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Mohinder Singh Gill v. CEC</b>: decided December 1977, reported 1978 (both years appear in the notes).'), ('mn', "Gill's Gap, Seshan's Seats, Baranwal's Board", '<b>Gap</b> = Gill: 324 fills gaps in law · <b>Seats</b> = Seshan: three equal seats, majority rules · <b>Board</b> = Baranwal: selection committee. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}], 24: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 326: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Kuldip Nayar v. Union of India</b> (2006 · 5 judges): open ballot for Rajya Sabha polls upheld; called the right to vote a statutory right'), ('mn', 'Sixty-one made Eighteen', 'the <b>61st</b> Amendment made the voting age <b>18</b>. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}], 36: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 330', 'b'), ('mn', 'M-L-L-P-T-N', '“<b>Most Leaders Like Power Too Nearly</b>”: Ministers 164(1) · <b>Lok Sabha 330</b> · Legislatures 332 · Panchayats 243D · Time limit 334 · NE States 371')], 'only': 'tb'}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 338A', 'b'), ('mn', 'C-P-I', '“<b>Commission Protects Indigenous peoples</b>”: Constitutional, Permanent, Independent. Numbers: Art 338A, Part XVI, 89th Am, 2003')], 'only': 'tb'}], 48: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 330', 'b'), ('mn', 'L-R-A-S', '“<b>Let Real Articles Speak</b>”: 81 Lok Sabha · 82 Readjustment · 170 Assemblies · <b>330, 332 SC/ST</b>')], 'only': 'tb'}], 37: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 338B: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Jaishri Laxmanrao Patil v. Chief Minister, Maharashtra</b> (2021 · 5 judges (3:2 on the 102nd Amendment point))', ["<b>Issue:</b> Maharashtra's SEBC Act, 2018 gave Marathas reservation (cut by the High Court to 12% in education and 13% in jobs), taking the total above 50%.", '<b>Held:</b> Maratha quota <b>struck down</b> unanimously: no exceptional circumstances to cross the <b>50% ceiling</b>, and Indra Sawhney need not be revisited. The 102nd Amendment was upheld, but by 3:2 the Court read it to mean <b>only the Centre (President) identifies SEBCs</b>. The <b>105th Amendment (2021)</b> reversed that reading.'], None, 24)]}], 38: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Mnemonic for Art 350A', 'b'), ('mn', 'M S H', '“<b>My State Hears</b>”: Mother tongue (350A) · Special Officer (350B) · Hindi (351). 350A and 350B were added together in 1956.')], 'only': 'tb'}], 52: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 350A: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>State of Karnataka v. Associated Management of (Govt. Recognised Unaided English Medium) Primary and Secondary Schools</b> (2014 · 5 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> A 1994 Karnataka order made Kannada or the mother tongue the compulsory medium in Classes 1 to 4, including in unaided schools.', "<b>Held:</b> No. <b>Mother tongue</b> (a term used only in Art. 350A) is the language of the child's linguistic group, as the <b>parent or guardian decides</b>. Choosing the medium is part of <b>Art. 19(1)(a)</b>; unaided schools may choose under 19(1)(g), minority schools under Art. 30. The order was struck down."], None, 24)]}], 22: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 352: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>59th Am, 1988</b>: through new Art 359A, allowed an Emergency in <b>Punjab</b> on 'internal disturbance'; repealed by the <b>63rd Am, 1989</b> (Art 352)")]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 356: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>State of Rajasthan v. Union of India</b> (1977 · 7 judges)', ["<b>Issue:</b> After the 1977 Lok Sabha polls the Janata government asked nine Congress-ruled States to seek dissolution; the States sued under Art. 131 to stop President's Rule.", '<b>Held:</b> Suits dismissed. The satisfaction is largely <b>political and subjective</b>; courts may step in only if it is <b>mala fide or rests on wholly extraneous grounds</b>. A narrow window of review.'], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>S.R. Bommai v. Union of India</b> (1994 · 9 judges)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Dismissals of the Nagaland (1988), Karnataka (1989, Bommai) and Meghalaya (1991) governments, and of the BJP governments of MP, Rajasthan and HP after the 1992 Babri demolition.', '<b>Held:</b> <b>Yes, reviewable</b>: satisfaction must rest on <b>relevant material</b>, which the Union must disclose; mala fide or extraneous grounds void it. Majority to be tested by a <b>floor test</b>; the Assembly is only <b>suspended, not dissolved</b>, until both Houses approve; the Court may <b>restore</b> a dismissed government. <b>Secularism and federalism are basic structure</b>. Nagaland, Karnataka and Meghalaya dismissals invalid; MP, Rajasthan, HP upheld (secularism).'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Union of India v. Harish Chandra Singh Rawat</b> (2016): Uttarakhand: the High Court quashed President's Rule; the SC ordered a supervised floor test, Rawat won and President's Rule was revoked"), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>In re Article 370 of the Constitution</b> (2023 · 5 judges): Parliament's power under 356(1)(b) is not only law-making; not every Union act during President's Rule can be challenged, but it must have a reasonable nexus with the proclamation"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>48th Am, 1984</b>: Punjab (1983 proclamation): continuance allowed up to <b>2 years</b> (Art 356)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>59th Am, 1988</b>: Punjab (1987 proclamation) freed from the cl (5) conditions; that proviso removed by the <b>63rd Am, 1989</b> (Art 356)'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>64th, 67th, 68th Ams, 1990-91</b>: Punjab limit stretched to <b>3½, 4 and then 5 years</b> (Art 356)')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 358: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>State of MP v. Thakur Bharat Singh (1967)</b>: Art 358 suspends Art 19 but does not validate executive action that has no backing of law.')]}, {'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 359: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Makhan Singh v. State of Punjab</b> (1964)', ['<b>Issue:</b> People detained under the Defence of India Rules during the 1962 Emergency, when a Presidential order had suspended the right to move court for Arts. 21 and 22.', '<b>Held:</b> It bars only pleas to enforce the <b>rights named in the order</b>. Pleas on other grounds, such as a <b>mala fide</b> exercise of power, stay open.'], None, 24), ('mn', "Khanna's lone No", 'ADM Jabalpur 4:1: <b>only Justice Khanna said No</b> to suspending habeas corpus; the 44th Am later took his side. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')]}], 9: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 368: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>I.C. Golaknath v. State of Punjab</b> (1967 · 11 judges, 6:5)', ['<b>Issue:</b> Punjab land-ceiling laws and the 1st, 4th and 17th Amendments were challenged.', "<b>Held:</b> Yes: an amendment is 'law'; <b>Parliament cannot take away or abridge FRs</b>. Shankari Prasad and Sajjan Singh overruled, but applied only for the future (<b>prospective overruling</b>), so past amendments survived."], None, 24), ('tree', '<b>Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India (NJAC case)</b> (2015 · 5 judges, 4:1)', ['<b>Issue:</b> The 99th Amendment and the NJAC Act, 2014 replaced the collegium with a commission including the Law Minister and two eminent persons.', '<b>Held:</b> <b>99th Amendment struck down</b>: <b>independence of the judiciary</b> (judicial primacy in appointments) is basic structure; collegium revived. Justice J. Chelameswar dissented.'], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu</b> (1992 · 5 judges): para 7 of the Tenth Schedule (barring courts) struck down because it was <b>not ratified by half the States</b>; rest upheld'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Union of India v. Rajendra N. Shah</b> (2021 · 3 judges, 2:1): Part IXB (97th Am) struck down for State cooperatives for want of ratification; it survives for multi-State societies')]}], 28: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 370: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>In re Article 370 of the Constitution</b> (2023 · 5 judges, unanimous (three concurring opinions))', ["<b>Issue:</b> Petitions challenged CO 272, CO 273 and the J&amp;K Reorganisation Act, 2019, all made while the State was under President's Rule.", "<b>Held:</b> Upheld. J&amp;K kept <b>no internal sovereignty</b> after accession; Art 370 was a <b>temporary, transitional provision</b> (asymmetric federalism). The <b>370(3) power survived</b> the Constituent Assembly's dissolution; State concurrence was not needed to apply the whole Constitution. But amending Art 370 <b>through Art 367 (CO 272) was invalid</b>, 'amendment through the back door'; this did not change the result. <b>Ladakh UT upheld</b> under Art 3; J&amp;K's change to a UT left open on the Union's promise to <b>restore statehood</b>; Assembly polls ordered by <b>30 September 2024</b>. Justice S.K. Kaul suggested a truth and reconciliation commission."], None, 24), ('bx', 'bl', "<b>Puranlal Lakhanpal v. President of India</b> (1961 · 5 judges): 'modification' under 370(1)(d) is wide enough for radical changes"), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>Sampat Prakash v. State of Jammu and Kashmir</b> (1968): Art 370 never ceased, as the Constituent Assembly never recommended it; the President could keep varying his orders'), ('bx', 'bl', '<b>State Bank of India v. Santosh Gupta</b> (2016): J&amp;K had no sovereignty outside the Constitution of India; the SARFAESI Act applies there'), ('mn', '272 Applies, 273 Ends', "<b>CO 272</b> (5 Aug 2019) applied the whole Constitution; <b>CO 273</b> (6 Aug 2019) made Art 370's clauses inoperative. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>")]}], 51: [{'after': '', 'nodes': [('sec', 'CARD · Art 392: cases, amendments, mnemonics', 'b'), ('tree', '<b>Art 392</b>', ['Let the President, by order, remove difficulties in moving from the Government of India Act, 1935 to the Constitution, by adapting its provisions, but only until the first Parliament met (1952). A transitional power, now spent.', "<b>392(1)</b>: President may adapt the Constitution by <b>modification, addition or omission</b> to remove difficulties, until Parliament's first meeting", '<b>392(2)</b>: Every such order laid before Parliament', '<b>392(3)</b>: Before 26 January 1950 this power (with Arts 324, 367(3) and 391) was used by the <b>Governor-General</b>'], None, 22)]}]}
